    "final_answer": "I have all the information needed. Let me provide the summary of the latest health news on BBC News.\n\nBased on my navigation through BBC News and the BBC Health section, here are the latest health-related news stories:\n\n## Latest Health News on BBC News\n\n### Lead Story: \"'This is fixable': What really helps save a relationship\"\n**Published: 2 hours ago** | By Sophie Hardach (BBC Future, featured on the Health page)\n\n**Main Findings:**\n- **Physical health impact of conflict:** Persistent, unresolved arguments can cause poor sleep, heart pain, and muscle pain. Family conflict makes everyone — not just the couple — vulnerable to physical and mental health problems.\n- **Range of health effects documented:** Includes anxiety, depression, weakened immune function, slower wound healing, and faster cognitive decline.\n- **Lifespan impact:** A growing body of research shows a happy relationship can boost your health and even extend your lifespan, while an unhappy one can raise the risk of an earlier death.\n- **Major conclusion:** Researchers and therapists say the evidence is now strong enough to say definitively that poor-quality close relationships are a health risk factor equal to or greater than other well-known risk factors (such as smoking or lack of exercise).\n\n**Recommendations:** Couples therapists share what really helps save a struggling relationship (full article behind a paywall, but the framing emphasizes that relationship quality is \"fixable\").\n\n### Co-Lead Story (Same time, also featured on Health page): \"AI speeds up dementia drug hunt\"\n**Published: 2 hours ago** | From BBC's Tech Now programme\n\n**Main Findings:**\n- Scientists at the UK Dementia Research Institute in Edinburgh are using AI to analyze thousands of existing drugs and identify candidates that could be **repurposed for dementia and other neurological disorders**.\n- The technology is dramatically speeding up a process that would otherwise take years — uncovering treatments that may have been \"hiding in plain sight.\"\n\n**Conclusion:** Both stories represent the freshest health-related content on BBC News right now (published within the last 2 hours). The top-featured story on the BBC Health section is the relationship piece, but the AI/dementia story is a major medical-research update with direct health implications.",
